   "source": [
    "# Home Practice: Interview-Style Questions\n",
    "\n",
    "1. What is the difference between a Series and a DataFrame?\n",
    "2. What does `df.info()` tell you? Why do we call it first?\n",
    "3. What is the difference between `iloc` and `loc`?\n",
    "4. Why do we use `&` and not `and` when we filter a DataFrame?\n",
    "5. What does `NaN` mean? Name two ways to deal with it.\n",
    "6. Does `df.sort_values(\"maths\")` change `df`? How do you keep the sorted result?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Extra:** Read `students.csv`. Print the name of the student with the highest Science mark. (Hint: sort, then use `.iloc[0]`.)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cell-80",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Key Takeaways\n",
    "\n",
    "- A **DataFrame** is a table. A **Series** is one column.\n",
    "- `pd.read_csv()` reads a file. Then use `head`, `shape`, `info` and `describe` to look at it.\n",
    "- `df[\"col\"]` = one column. `df[[\"a\", \"b\"]]` = many columns.\n",
    "- `iloc` = by position. `loc` = by label.\n",
    "- Filter with `df[condition]`. Use `&`, `|` with brackets.\n",
    "- `sort_values` sorts. `ascending=False` = big to small.\n",
    "- `isna().sum()` counts missing values. `dropna()` removes them. `fillna()` fills them.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Next class:** Pandas II: groupby, merge and new columns."
   ]
  }
